A few ideas I had:
1) Set up a central webserver, with which all Vulture/NetHack installations talk, that coordinates acceptance/consumption of bones and scores.
Most promising, I think.  Could even be done w/o modifying Vulture or NetHack source by doing something similar to Hearse, provided users are trusted and some concurrency issues can be tolerated (e.g. chance of duplicate consumption of bones).

2) Install Vulture on the machine running NetHack with a shared playground, then use SSH X-forwarding to let remote users play Vulture.
-Unfortunately Vulture appears unplayably laggy doing this, even when attempting to optimize using compression and a faster SSH cipher.  Not sure if there are more tweaks that could be done.

3) Modify Vulture source to decouple it from its built-in NetHack code, instead having it drive a remote NetHack installation via interpreting text coming across an SSH session.
-Probably a large undertaking and seems inherently inefficient; the real "multiplayer" aspects of the game are just the high scores and stumbling upon another user's bones.

